// Five-Card Draw — heads-up fixed limit (2 players, ONE hand) — formalized DSL
// (parser/checker/runtime target).
//
// Companion to five-card-draw.md. The corpus's first DRAW-family poker game:
// where Stud and Hold'em add cards, this one lets a player exchange them.
//
// EXACT VARIANT. Two players — Pagat's stated minimum, and the count at which
// a hand's exchange is decided by one opponent's draw rather than averaged
// over a table. Ante 1 each, no blinds (Pagat: "before the deal each player
// puts an agreed ante into the pot"). Five cards each, dealt one at a time. A betting round; then each
// player in turn discards from zero to three cards face down and is dealt that
// many replacements; then a second betting round; then the showdown, best
// five-card poker hand taking the pot. One hand, then the game ends; the score
// is the hand's chip delta.
//
// Source: Pagat, https://www.pagat.com/poker/variants/5draw.html.
//
// THREE PARAMETERS THIS VARIANT SETS, because Pagat leaves each open:
//
// - FIXED LIMIT 2 before the draw, 4 after. The page names no bet sizing at
//   all, so the doubling at the draw is this variant's, chosen to match the
//   doubling at the turn in the corpus's fixed-limit Hold'em.
// - FOUR AGGRESSIVE ACTIONS per street (a bet and three raises) — the same cap
//   Seven-Card Stud declares. `raise_cap` is where the family keeps it, so the
//   number is this game's and the mechanism is the library's.
// - DISCARD ZERO TO THREE, the page's base rule. Its optional extension —
//   "some allow a player to discard and draw four cards if the fifth card is
//   an ace" — is a house rule this variant does not adopt, so `toss`'s guard
//   is a flat three and no seat's holding widens it.
//
// ONE DEVIATION from the source, named here rather than left for a reader to
// discover: THE SECOND ROUND OPENS WITH THE SAME SEAT AS THE FIRST. Pagat
// opens the post-draw round "with the player who opened the betting on the
// first round" — the seat that made the first round's opening AGGRESSION, not
// its first-to-act. That seat is not derivable from anything this game can
// read: `bet_by` is cleared by `open_street`, and equal `committed` totals do
// not say who bet first. The family library (docs/libraries/
// poker_betting.cardlang) owns `bet` and `raise` and writes no opener, so a
// game cannot observe the aggression without changing the library, and `uses`
// imports without inheriting — there is no override. Both rounds therefore
// open with the seat to the dealer's left, which is also what a checked-around
// first round yields under Pagat's own rule. A language gap, not a tidy-up:
// issue #544, whose witness this game is.
//
// Pagat's SHOWDOWN ORDER wants the same unrecorded fact — players show "in
// clockwise order, beginning with the last player who took aggressive action
// (bet or raised) in the second betting round". It is not a second deviation:
// every contender shows, and no decision stands between the two reveals, so
// the order changes no seat's information at any decision and the showdown
// below reveals both hands at once, as Stud's and Hold'em's do.
//
// THE DISCARD IS A SEQUENCE OF ONE-CARD DECISIONS, which is the documented
// form of `move chosen N cards` here and not a departure from the game. The
// one-decision spelling (`move chosen some cards from hole[actor] where
// jointly ...`) needs a registered per-predicate subset codec, which is engine
// Python; the announce-then-stage decomposition is what decisions.md,
// "Joint-predicate selection" prescribes in its place. `toss` discards one
// chosen card and `stand` ends the exchange, and the `turns` form's `again`
// axis keeps the same player deciding until they stand — so a player completes
// their whole exchange before the next seat begins and learns nothing between
// their own tosses. The reachable exchanges and the information each seat
// holds are those of the one-motion push at the table; only the shape of the
// game tree differs.
//
// THE DRAW COUNT IS PUBLIC AND THE DISCARDS ARE NOT — the whole strategic
// content of the exchange. `discards[player] : HiddenPile<player>` is that
// sentence as a zone type: identity to the owner, count_only to everyone else.
// A seat that draws three tells the table it drew three and nothing more, and
// nobody had to write an observation rule to make it so.
//
// NO PLAYER IS EVER ALL-IN, and that is arithmetic, not luck: four aggressions
// at 2 cap the first round's `bet_to_match` at 8, four at 4 cap the second at
// 16, so a hand cannot take more than 24 chips plus the 1-chip ante off a
// 100-chip stack. `can_act`'s `stack[p] > 0` therefore never gates, no side pot
// can form, and the ante is never a partial post.
//
// THE DECK NEVER RUNS SHORT: 10 cards dealt and at most 6 drawn, out of 52, so
// the reshuffle rule for a depleted stock (Pagat) has no reachable case here
// and is not written.
//
// Betting proceeds as in standard fixed-limit poker: check/bet/call/raise and
// the can_act/owes/pending ring predicates come from the family library
// (docs/libraries/poker_betting.cardlang), whose membership is a game's own
// `uses` line and is listed nowhere. `fold` is this game's own — the folded
// hand mucks unseen, as it does wherever the family's `fold` is written.
//
// Chips are plain integers (the Stud/Leduc convention). `net[player]` is the
// terminal score — the hand's chip delta against the starting stack.

game FiveCardDraw {

  uses poker_betting

  players: 2
  direction: clockwise
  max_length: 200

  cards: standard52
  ranking: aces high

  // The one borrowing from outside the DSL, and it is the family's own, not a
  // game-local one: `pot_share` is the poker showdown query Seven-Card Stud
  // declares too. Declaring the shared name rather than minting a second
  // binding of the same query is deliberate — a name per variant is the debt
  // issue #232 exists to remove, and nothing pins a declared Primitive to one
  // game: each block is checked against its own declarations. The settlement
  // arithmetic has one home either way (`cardlang/runtime/poker.py`), and what
  // a game contributes is which cards a contender shows, which for this game
  // is exactly what the reads below name.
  //
  // Every read is WHOLE, none narrowed to the argument — the
  // settlement ranks every entrant's holding against every other's and layers
  // the pot by commitment, so it needs all the hands and all the commitments;
  // `p` picks which share of that settlement comes back, not which cards go
  // into it. It reads the contenders' cards from `hole` and `upcards`
  // together, which is why the showdown reveal below moves them between those
  // two zones rather than to a third.
  primitives {
    pot_share(p : Player) : Integer
        reads committed, folded, in_hand, hole, upcards
  }

  zones {
    deck             : Deck
    hole[player]     : Hand<player>        // the five private cards
    upcards[player]  : PublicHand<player>  // the showdown reveal
    discards[player] : HiddenPile<player>  // the exchange: count public, faces not
    muck             : Muck                // folded / spent cards
  }

  state {
    // Ample by the 25-chip bound argued in the header: no seat can be short.
    stack[player]     : Integer = 100
    net[player]       : Integer = 0        // terminal score: chips won or lost

    // The showdown primitive's declared reads (the block above). Both seats
    // always enter — there is exactly one hand — so `in_hand` is constant.
    in_hand[player]   : Boolean = true

    // The library's contract (poker_betting `requires`). `acted` and `limit`
    // are absent because the library provides them; the bet sizes are named at
    // the streets that use them.
    committed[player] : Integer = 0
    bet_by[player]    : Integer = 0
    folded[player]    : Boolean = false
    bet_to_match      : Integer = 0
    level             : Integer = 0
    raises            : Integer = 0
    raise_cap         : Integer = 4        // four aggressions per street, both streets

    // Seat 1 deals, so seat 0 is "the player nearest the dealer's left" and
    // opens the deal, the exchange and both betting rounds. Public — which
    // seat holds the button is visible to everyone at the table.
    first_actor       : Player = 0

    // The exchange. `tossed` is how many cards a seat has pushed forward this
    // hand and is what `stand` deals back; `drawn` retires a seat from the
    // exchange loop; `more` is the `turns` form's go-again flag, set by `toss`
    // so the same seat keeps deciding until it stands.
    tossed[player]    : Integer = 0
    drawn[player]     : Boolean = false
    more              : Boolean = false
  }

  phase deal {
    shuffle deck

    // Antes: forced, equal, and posted after the street is opened, since
    // opening clears `bet_by`. An ante is not a bet — it buys no right to be
    // called — so it moves `committed` and the stack and leaves `bet_to_match`
    // and `level` at zero, which is what makes the first round open with a
    // check available to both seats.
    //
    // The two statements are hand-written because the family library brings no
    // procedure for a forced bet, only for opening a street — so every poker
    // game in the corpus writes its own ante, blind or bring-in, and the two
    // shapes a designer would copy differ in whether the post IS the street's
    // bet. This game is that duplication's named witness: issue #548.
    run open_street(2)
    for each player p: stack[p] := stack[p] - 1
    for each player p: committed[p] := committed[p] + 1

    // Five passes of one card, not one pass of five: the cards go round the
    // table one at a time. Each pass starts at seat 0, which here is exactly
    // where the rules start it — the dealer is seat 1, so seat 0 is the seat
    // to the dealer's left.
    for each player p: deal 1 card from deck to hole[p]
    for each player p: deal 1 card from deck to hole[p]
    for each player p: deal 1 card from deck to hole[p]
    for each player p: deal 1 card from deck to hole[p]
    for each player p: deal 1 card from deck to hole[p]
  }

  // The first betting round, opened by the seat to the dealer's left. No forced
  // bet stands, so both seats may check it round.
  phase first_betting {
    round offering [check, bet, call, fold, raise] from first_actor
          over players where pending(player)
          until (number of players where pending(player)) is 0
             or ((number of players where can_act(player)) <= 1
                 and (number of players where can_act(player) and owes(player)) is 0)
  }

  // The exchange. One seat at a time, in turn order from the dealer's left: it
  // tosses up to three cards face down and then stands, taking that many
  // replacements. `again more` is what keeps the seat deciding — a `toss` sets
  // the flag and the form re-offers the same player, so no seat sees another's
  // exchange partway through its own. A hand that ended in a fold skips this
  // entirely.
  phase draw {
    if (number of players where not folded[player]) > 1 {
      // The first street is closed and the second opens at the doubled limit
      // before a card is exchanged — which is where the table opens it: from
      // the moment the first round closes no bet stands, nobody owes and
      // nobody has acted on the round to come. Opening it after the exchange
      // instead, where every other poker game in the corpus has its
      // `open_street`, would leave the closed round's standing bet and
      // aggression count reading as a live street all through the draw. The
      // library opens streets and closes none, so that placement is the
      // family's to settle rather than this game's: issue #639.
      run open_street(4)
      turns d from first_actor
            over players where not folded[player] and not drawn[player]
            until (number of players where not folded[player]
                                       and not drawn[player]) is 0
            again more {
        offer to d one of [toss, stand]
      }
    }
  }

  // The second betting round, on the street the draw opened, at the doubled
  // limit. Pagat begins it with the first round's opening aggressor; this game
  // begins it with the same seat as the first round — the header's one
  // deviation, issue #544.
  phase second_betting {
    if (number of players where not folded[player]) > 1 {
      round offering [check, bet, call, fold, raise] from first_actor
            over players where pending(player)
            until (number of players where pending(player)) is 0
               or ((number of players where can_act(player)) <= 1
                   and (number of players where can_act(player) and owes(player)) is 0)
    }
  }

  // Showdown — RNG-free, decision-free. A contested hand reveals both players'
  // five cards into a public hand (the movement event carries both identities —
  // the derived reveal); a hand that ended in a fold reveals nothing, which is
  // what keeps a folded holding unknowable even in hindsight. The reveal moves
  // `hole` into `upcards` because `pot_share` reads the two together, so the
  // contenders' cards are the same five before and after it.
  phase showdown {
    if (number of players where not folded[player]) > 1 {
      for each player p: if not folded[p] {
        move all cards from hole[p] to upcards[p]
      }
    }
    for each player p: stack[p] := stack[p] + pot_share(p)
    for each player p: move all cards from hole[p] to muck
    for each player p: move all cards from upcards[p] to muck
    // The exchanged cards are spent when the hand is; they were never seen and
    // the Muck keeps them that way.
    for each player p: move all cards from discards[p] to muck
    for each player p: net[p] := stack[p] - 100
  }

  winner: highest net
}

// This game's own betting move. check/bet/call/raise and the can_act/owes/
// pending ring predicates come from `uses poker_betting` above; `fold` stays
// here because it touches this game's zones. The folded hand goes to the Muck —
// trivial to every observer, the owner included — so a fold reveals nothing,
// matching the rule that a folded hand is never shown. All five are offered
// together in `offering [check, bet, call, fold, raise]` order; the `when:`
// guards filter to the exact option list per decision: free-to-act ->
// [check(, bet)], facing a bet -> [call, fold(, raise)].

move_type fold {
  when: bet_to_match > bet_by[actor]
  effect {
    folded[actor] := true
    move all cards from hole[actor] to muck
  }
}

// The exchange vocabulary. `toss` pushes one card forward, face down, and
// keeps the turn; `stand` ends the exchange and takes the replacements. Three
// is the ceiling Pagat states ("a player can discard from zero to three
// cards"), and it is the `when:` guard rather than a count the seat announces,
// so a seat holding three tossed cards is offered only `stand` and the
// decision point always has a legal move.

move_type toss {
  when: tossed[actor] < 3
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hole[actor] to discards[actor]
    tossed[actor] := tossed[actor] + 1
    more := true
  }
}

// Standing with nothing tossed is standing pat; standing with three tossed is
// drawing three. The same decision either way — the seat is done exchanging —
// and the replacements arrive at that moment, as the dealer deals them once the
// discard is complete.
move_type stand {
  effect {
    drawn[actor] := true
    if tossed[actor] > 0 {
      deal tossed[actor] cards from deck to hole[actor]
    }
  }
}
